#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/performance/06-output-variables.ipynb:70
msgid "Solution storage and output variables"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/performance/06-output-variables.ipynb:72
msgid ""
"A PyBaMM model is defined as a set of differential equations. For an "
"ordinary differential equation (ODE) model, the model is defined as"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/performance/06-output-variables.ipynb:74
msgid ""
"\\begin{align*}\n"
"\\frac{d\\mathbf{y}}{dt} = \\mathbf{f}(\\mathbf{y}, t)\n"
"\\end{align*}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/performance/06-output-variables.ipynb:81
msgid ""
"where :math:`\\mathbf{y}` is the state vector, and :math:`\\mathbf{f}` is "
"the vector of ODEs (PyBaMM actually uses a semi-explicit DAE equation form, "
"but for the purposes of this notebook we can just assume we're dealing with "
"an ODE). The state vector :math:`\\mathbf{y}` contains all the variables "
"that are integrated over time by the solver."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/performance/06-output-variables.ipynb:83
msgid ""
"For example, we can create a DFN model with PyBaMM and look at each of the "
"state variables individually and all together in the final concatenated "
"state vector :math:`\\mathbf{y}`:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/performance/06-output-variables.ipynb:-1
msgid "[2]:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/performance/06-output-variables.ipynb:-1
msgid ""
"model = pybamm.lithium_ion.DFN()\n"
"print(\"The model state variables are:\", [var.name for var in model.rhs])\n"
"sim = pybamm.Simulation(model)\n"
"sim.build()\n"
"print(\n"
"    \"The concatenated state vector is a vector of shape\",\n"
"    sim.built_model.concatenated_rhs.shape,\n"
")"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/performance/06-output-variables.ipynb:111
msgid ""
"<pre>\n"
"The model state variables are: [&#39;Discharge capacity [A.h]&#39;, &#39;"
"Throughput capacity [A.h]&#39;, &#39;Negative particle concentration [mol."
"m-3]&#39;, &#39;Positive particle concentration [mol.m-3]&#39;, &#39;"
"Porosity times concentration [mol.m-3]&#39;]\n"
"The concatenated state vector is a vector of shape (862, 1)\n"
"</pre>"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/performance/06-output-variables.ipynb:118
msgid ""
"\\begin{sphinxVerbatim}[commandchars=\\\\\\{\\}]\n"
"The model state variables are: ['Discharge capacity [A.h]', 'Throughput "
"capacity [A.h]', 'Negative particle concentration [mol.m-3]', 'Positive "
"particle concentration [mol.m-3]', 'Porosity times concentration [mol."
"m-3]']\n"
"The concatenated state vector is a vector of shape (862, 1)\n"
"\\end{sphinxVerbatim}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/performance/06-output-variables.ipynb:126
msgid ""
"The model state variables are: ['Discharge capacity [A.h]', 'Throughput "
"capacity [A.h]', 'Negative particle concentration [mol.m-3]', 'Positive "
"particle concentration [mol.m-3]', 'Porosity times concentration [mol."
"m-3]']\n"
"The concatenated state vector is a vector of shape (862, 1)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/performance/06-output-variables.ipynb:138
msgid ""
"The user is also interested in a number of output variables :math:"
"`\\mathbf{g}`. Once the simulation is complete and the model has been solved "
"to get :math:`\\mathbf{y}`, the output variables :math:`\\mathbf{g}` can be "
"calculated from :math:`\\mathbf{y}` using a function :math:`\\mathbf{h}`:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/performance/06-output-variables.ipynb:140
msgid ""
"\\begin{align*}\n"
"\\mathbf{g} = \\mathbf{h}(\\mathbf{y})\n"
"\\end{align*}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/performance/06-output-variables.ipynb:147
msgid ""
"where :math:`\\mathbf{h}` is a function that extracts the variables of "
"interest from the state vector. Each model in PyBaMM has many such variables "
"of interest, and the user can choose which ones to extract and plot. E.g. "
"perhaps the user wants to solve the DFN model and plot the positive "
"electrode capacity? In this case, the expression for the variable of "
"interest is a function :math:`h` of the state vector :math:`\\mathbf{y}`, "
"and this function is defined in the DFN model using a PyBaMM expression tree:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/performance/06-output-variables.ipynb:-1
msgid "[3]:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/performance/06-output-variables.ipynb:-1
msgid "print(model.variables[\"Positive electrode capacity [A.h]\"])"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/performance/06-output-variables.ipynb:169
msgid ""
"<pre>\n"
"0.0002777777777777778 * yz-average(x-average(Positive electrode active "
"material volume fraction)) * Positive electrode thickness [m] * Electrode "
"width [m] * Electrode height [m] * Number of electrodes connected in "
"parallel to make a cell * Maximum concentration in positive electrode [mol."
"m-3] * F\n"
"</pre>"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/performance/06-output-variables.ipynb:175
msgid ""
"\\begin{sphinxVerbatim}[commandchars=\\\\\\{\\}]\n"
"0.0002777777777777778 * yz-average(x-average(Positive electrode active "
"material volume fraction)) * Positive electrode thickness [m] * Electrode "
"width [m] * Electrode height [m] * Number of electrodes connected in "
"parallel to make a cell * Maximum concentration in positive electrode [mol."
"m-3] * F\n"
"\\end{sphinxVerbatim}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/performance/06-output-variables.ipynb:182
msgid ""
"0.0002777777777777778 * yz-average(x-average(Positive electrode active "
"material volume fraction)) * Positive electrode thickness [m] * Electrode "
"width [m] * Electrode height [m] * Number of electrodes connected in "
"parallel to make a cell * Maximum concentration in positive electrode [mol."
"m-3] * F"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/performance/06-output-variables.ipynb:193
msgid ""
"PyBaMM doesn't evaluate this expression until the user asks for it when they "
"extract it from the solution object. Instead, it stores the solution vector :"
"math:`\\mathbf{y}` at each time point, and then evaluates the expression for "
"the variable of interest :math:`\\mathbf{g}` at each time point when the "
"user asks for it. In the code below, the evaluation of the positive "
"electrode capacity is done by calling ``solution[\"Positive electrode "
"capacity [A.h]\"]``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/performance/06-output-variables.ipynb:-1
msgid "[4]:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/performance/06-output-variables.ipynb:-1
msgid ""
"# Solve the model, storing the state vector at each time step\n"
"solution = sim.solve([0, 3600])\n"
"\n"
"# Extract the positive electrode capacity using the function $h(y)$ and the "
"stored state vector $y$\n"
"pos_elec_capacity = solution[\"Positive electrode capacity [A.h]\"]"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/performance/06-output-variables.ipynb:218
msgid ""
"During a solve, the PyBaMM solver needs to store the solution vector :math:"
"`\\mathbf{y}` at each time step. By default, PyBaMM stores the entire "
"vector, so if the state vector is of size :math:`n` and the solver steps "
"through :math:`m` time points, the memory usage is :math:`O(nm)`. This can "
"be a problem for large models, especially when running on a machine with "
"limited memory, or when running a very long-running simulation that needs to "
"store many time points."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/performance/06-output-variables.ipynb:221
msgid "Calculating output variables on-the-fly"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/performance/06-output-variables.ipynb:223
msgid ""
"However, it is likely that the user is only interested in a small subset of "
"the variables, and that the size of these output variables :math:`\\mathbf{g}"
"` is much smaller than the size of the state vector :math:`\\mathbf{y}`. "
"Therefore, it could be wasteful to store the entire state vector :math:"
"`\\mathbf{y}`. To address this, PyBaMM has a feature called \"output "
"variables\". This allows the user to specify a list of variables that they "
"are interested in up front (i.e. before the solve). During the solve, PyBaMM "
"will evaluate the output variables of interest at each time point and "
"discard the rest of the state vector. At the end of the solve, the ``pybamm."
"Solution`` object will only contain the output variables of interest, as "
"well as the full state vector at the last time point (so that other "
"simulations can be run from this point)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/performance/06-output-variables.ipynb:226
msgid ""
"Let's see how this works in practice. We'll start by solving a DFN model "
"with a long-running experimental protocol, and assume we are only interested "
"in the terminal voltage. We setup a PyBaMM simulation as normal and solve "
"it, and once the solver has finished we extract the terminal voltage from "
"the solution object. To evaluate the memory usage of this simulation, we "
"will use the ``tracemalloc`` library, which allows us to track the total "
"amount of memory allocated by the Python interpreter. We'll also keep track "
"of the time taken to solve the model using the ``time`` library."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/performance/06-output-variables.ipynb:-1
msgid "[5]:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/performance/06-output-variables.ipynb:-1
msgid ""
"model = pybamm.lithium_ion.DFN()\n"
"solver = pybamm.IDAKLUSolver()\n"
"experiment = pybamm.Experiment(\n"
"    [\n"
"        \"Discharge at 0.1 A for 1 hour\",\n"
"        \"Charge at 0.1 A for 1 hour\",\n"
"    ]\n"
"    * 100\n"
")\n"
"\n"
"tracemalloc.start()\n"
"sim = pybamm.Simulation(model, solver=solver, experiment=experiment)\n"
"time_start = time.perf_counter()\n"
"sol = sim.solve()\n"
"t_eval = np.linspace(0, 3600 * 10, 100)\n"
"voltage = sol[\"Terminal voltage [V]\"](t_eval)\n"
"time_end = time.perf_counter()\n"
"print(\"Time to solve: \", time_end - time_start)\n"
"\n"
"snapshot = tracemalloc.take_snapshot()\n"
"top_stats = snapshot.statistics(\"lineno\")\n"
"total_size = sum(stat.size for stat in top_stats)\n"
"print(\"Total allocated size: %.1f MB\" % (total_size / 10**6))\n"
"tracemalloc.stop()"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/performance/06-output-variables.ipynb:271
msgid ""
"<pre>\n"
"Time to solve:  2.17429095800253\n"
"Total allocated size: 11.6 MB\n"
"</pre>"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/performance/06-output-variables.ipynb:278
msgid ""
"\\begin{sphinxVerbatim}[commandchars=\\\\\\{\\}]\n"
"Time to solve:  2.17429095800253\n"
"Total allocated size: 11.6 MB\n"
"\\end{sphinxVerbatim}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/performance/06-output-variables.ipynb:286
msgid ""
"Time to solve:  2.17429095800253\n"
"Total allocated size: 11.6 MB"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/performance/06-output-variables.ipynb:298
msgid ""
"So for about 10 seconds of compute we have already generated 202 MB of data "
"that is in memory, for longer-running simulations this can quickly become a "
"problem. We will now use the output variables feature to reduce the memory "
"usage. When the solver is created, we pass in a list of output variables "
"that we are interested in. The solver will then only store these variables "
"at each time point, rather than the entire state vector."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/performance/06-output-variables.ipynb:-1
msgid "[6]:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/performance/06-output-variables.ipynb:-1
msgid ""
"import time\n"
"import tracemalloc\n"
"\n"
"import numpy as np\n"
"\n"
"import pybamm\n"
"\n"
"model = pybamm.lithium_ion.DFN()\n"
"solver = pybamm.IDAKLUSolver(output_variables=[\"Voltage [V]\"])\n"
"experiment = pybamm.Experiment(\n"
"    [\n"
"        \"Discharge at 0.1 A for 1 hour\",\n"
"        \"Charge at 0.1 A for 1 hour\",\n"
"    ]\n"
"    * 100\n"
")\n"
"\n"
"tracemalloc.start()\n"
"sim = pybamm.Simulation(model, solver=solver, experiment=experiment)\n"
"time_start = time.perf_counter()\n"
"sol = sim.solve()\n"
"t_eval = np.linspace(0, 3600 * 10, 100)\n"
"voltage = sol[\"Voltage [V]\"](t_eval)\n"
"time_end = time.perf_counter()\n"
"print(\"Time to solve: \", time_end - time_start)\n"
"\n"
"snapshot = tracemalloc.take_snapshot()\n"
"top_stats = snapshot.statistics(\"lineno\")\n"
"total_size = sum(stat.size for stat in top_stats)\n"
"print(\"Total allocated size: %.1f MB\" % (total_size / 10**6))\n"
"tracemalloc.stop()"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/performance/06-output-variables.ipynb:349
msgid ""
"<pre>\n"
"Time to solve:  2.2499296669993782\n"
"Total allocated size: 14.7 MB\n"
"</pre>"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/performance/06-output-variables.ipynb:356
msgid ""
"\\begin{sphinxVerbatim}[commandchars=\\\\\\{\\}]\n"
"Time to solve:  2.2499296669993782\n"
"Total allocated size: 14.7 MB\n"
"\\end{sphinxVerbatim}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/performance/06-output-variables.ipynb:364
msgid ""
